### Why does thenApply produce nested CF<CF<T>>?

thenApply wraps whatever the function returns inside a CompletableFuture. If the function returns a plain Integer, you get CF<Integer>. If the function returns CF<Integer> (another async call), thenApply wraps that CF in another CF, giving CF<CF<Integer>>.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class NestedCFDemo {
    static CompletableFuture<Integer> fetchBalanceAsync(String accountId) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 150000);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: thenApply with async function produces NESTED CF
        CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<Integer>> nested =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "SAV2001")
                .thenApply(id -> fetchBalanceAsync(id)); // returns CF<Integer>
        // Result: CF<CF<Integer>>! Needs: nested.join().join()

        // CORRECT: thenCompose flattens to single CF
        CompletableFuture<Integer> flat =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "SAV2001")
                .thenCompose(id -> fetchBalanceAsync(id)); // returns CF<Integer>
        // Result: CF<Integer>! Needs: flat.join()

        System.out.println("Balance: Rs." + flat.join());
    }
}
```

```text
Balance: Rs.150000
```

### How does this relate to Stream map() vs flatMap()?

The concept is identical. map() wraps the return value in the container. flatMap() unwraps an inner container.

```java
// Stream analogy:
// map:     Stream<String> -> map(s -> List.of(s)) -> Stream<List<String>> NESTED
// flatMap: Stream<String> -> flatMap(s -> List.of(s).stream()) -> Stream<String> FLAT

// CompletableFuture analogy:
// thenApply:   CF<String> -> thenApply(s -> asyncCall(s))   -> CF<CF<Integer>> NESTED
// thenCompose: CF<String> -> thenCompose(s -> asyncCall(s)) -> CF<Integer>     FLAT
```

### What is the decision rule?

| Function returns | Use | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Plain value (T) | thenApply | CF<T> (correct) |
| CompletableFuture<T> | thenCompose | CF<T> (correct) |
| CompletableFuture<T> | thenApply (wrong!) | CF<CF<T>> (nested!) |

```java
// Function returns String (plain value) -> thenApply
cf.thenApply(s -> s.toUpperCase());              // CF<String>

// Function returns Integer (plain value) -> thenApply
cf.thenApply(s -> Integer.parseInt(s));          // CF<Integer>

// Function returns CF<Integer> (async call) -> thenCompose
cf.thenCompose(id -> fetchBalanceAsync(id));     // CF<Integer>

// WRONG: function returns CF but using thenApply
cf.thenApply(id -> fetchBalanceAsync(id));       // CF<CF<Integer>>!
```

### What happens with deeply nested pipelines?

If you use thenApply for every async step, the nesting grows with each step.

```java
// WRONG: three thenApply calls with async functions
CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<String>>> deep =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "SAV2001")
        .thenApply(id -> fetchProfileAsync(id))       // CF<CF<Profile>>
        .thenApply(cfProfile -> /* how to use this? */);
// Requires: deep.join().join().join() -- unworkable!

// CORRECT: thenCompose keeps it flat
CompletableFuture<String> flat = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> "SAV2001")
    .thenCompose(id -> fetchProfileAsync(id))         // CF<Profile>
    .thenCompose(profile -> fetchScoreAsync(profile)) // CF<Integer>
    .thenApply(score -> "Score: " + score);           // CF<String>
// flat.join() returns String directly
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: thenApply with async function
cf.thenApply(id -> fetchAsync(id)); // CF<CF<T>> nested!

// CORRECT: thenCompose with async function
cf.thenCompose(id -> fetchAsync(id)); // CF<T> flat!

// WRONG: mixing up when to use which
cf.thenCompose(s -> CompletableFuture.completedFuture(s.toUpperCase()));
// Works but unnecessarily creates a CF. Use thenApply:
cf.thenApply(s -> s.toUpperCase()); // simpler, no extra CF
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Is thenCompose equivalent to thenApply followed by unwrapping? Conceptually yes, but thenCompose avoids creating the nested CF in the first place.
- Can you use thenCompose when the function returns a plain value? No, thenCompose expects the function to return a CompletableFuture. Use thenApply for plain values.
- What is the performance difference? thenCompose avoids creating an extra CompletableFuture wrapper, so it has slightly less overhead.
